The two arrays can be split so that exactly `k` values are on the left side. The correct split is found with binary search on the shorter array. Once every value on the left is no bigger than every value on the right, the largest left value is the answer.

The search limits also make sure we do not take more than `k` values from one array or leave too few values for the other array.

```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <climits>
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;

class Solution {
public:
    int kthElement(vector<int> &a, vector<int>& b, int k) {
        if (a.size() > b.size()) {
            return kthElement(b, a, k);
        }

        int n = static_cast<int>(a.size());
        int m = static_cast<int>(b.size());
        int low = max(0, k - m);
        int high = min(k, n);

        while (low <= high) {
            int takeA = low + (high - low) / 2;
            int takeB = k - takeA;

            int leftA = (takeA == 0) ? INT_MIN : a[takeA - 1];
            int leftB = (takeB == 0) ? INT_MIN : b[takeB - 1];
            int rightA = (takeA == n) ? INT_MAX : a[takeA];
            int rightB = (takeB == m) ? INT_MAX : b[takeB];

            if (leftA <= rightB && leftB <= rightA) {
                return max(leftA, leftB);
            }

            if (leftA > rightB) {
                high = takeA - 1;
            } else {
                low = takeA + 1;
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }
};
```

The input arrays are only read. The `INT_MIN` and `INT_MAX` values act as boundaries when a partition touches an array end.

Edge cases such as `k = 1`, `k` equal to the total length, or one empty array are covered by the partition limits.

Time complexity: `O(log(min(n, m)))`
Space complexity: `O(1)`

This version throws away a group of values at each call instead of building a merged array. Compare the value around the middle of the next `k` values in both arrays. The smaller one, along with all values before it in that array, cannot be the answer, so those values can be skipped.

A dry run with `a = [1, 4, 7]`, `b = [2, 3, 9]`, and `k = 4` first compares `1` and `2`, skips `1`, then continues with `k = 3`. The next comparisons eventually leave `4` as the fourth value.

```java
class Solution {
    public int kthElement(int[] a, int[] b, int k) {
        return find(a, 0, b, 0, k);
    }

    private int find(int[] a, int startA, int[] b, int startB, int k) {
        if (startA == a.length) {
            return b[startB + k - 1];
        }
        if (startB == b.length) {
            return a[startA + k - 1];
        }
        if (k == 1) {
            return Math.min(a[startA], b[startB]);
        }

        int half = k / 2;
        int indexA = Math.min(startA + half - 1, a.length - 1);
        int indexB = Math.min(startB + half - 1, b.length - 1);

        int countA = indexA - startA + 1;
        int countB = indexB - startB + 1;

        if (a[indexA] <= b[indexB]) {
            return find(a, indexA + 1, b, startB, k - countA);
        }
        return find(a, startA, b, indexB + 1, k - countB);
    }
}
```

The recursion stops quickly when one array is exhausted or when only one position remains. The input arrays are not modified. The method expects a valid 1-based `k`.

Time complexity: `O(log k)`
Space complexity: `O(log k)` for the recursive call stack

A small heap gives another direct way to merge the arrays. Put the first item from each non-empty array into the heap, remove the smallest item, and add the next item from the same array. After removing `k - 1` items, the next item is the answer.

For `[1, 4, 9]` and `[2, 3, 8]`, the heap removes `1`, `2`, and `3` first. When `k = 4`, the next removed value is `4`.

```java
import java.util.PriorityQueue;

class Solution {
    public int kthElement(int[] a, int[] b, int k) {
        PriorityQueue<int[]> heap = new PriorityQueue<>((x, y) -> Integer.compare(x[0], y[0]));

        if (a.length > 0) {
            heap.offer(new int[] {a[0], 0, 0});
        }
        if (b.length > 0) {
            heap.offer(new int[] {b[0], 1, 0});
        }

        int answer = 0;

        for (int removed = 0; removed < k; removed++) {
            int[] current = heap.poll();
            answer = current[0];

            int arrayId = current[1];
            int index = current[2] + 1;

            if (arrayId == 0 && index < a.length) {
                heap.offer(new int[] {a[index], 0, index});
            } else if (arrayId == 1 && index < b.length) {
                heap.offer(new int[] {b[index], 1, index});
            }
        }

        return answer;
    }
}
```

The heap always contains at most two entries because there are only two input arrays. The arrays stay unchanged. This is easier to follow than the partition method, but it is slower for very large inputs.

Time complexity: `O(k log 2)`, which is `O(k)` here
Space complexity: `O(1)` extra space because the heap has at most two items

## Intuition
Both arrays are already sorted, so we can use the two-pointer merge technique without actually merging the entire arrays.

- i points to the current element of arr1.
- j points to the current element of arr2.
- At each step, compare arr1[i] and arr2[j].
- Take the smaller element and increment its pointer.
- Keep doing this until we reach the k-th element.

Since we only need the k-th element, there is no need to create a new merged array or process elements after the k-th position.

## Approach
1. Initialize two pointers:
   - i = 0
   - j = 0
2. Repeat k times:
   - If arr1[i] <= arr2[j], take arr1[i] and increment i.
   - Otherwise, take arr2[j] and increment j.
3. The element selected on the k-th iteration is the answer.
4. Handle the case where one array gets exhausted by taking elements from the other array.

## Solution
```java[]
class Solution {
    public int kthElement(int[] a, int[] b, int k) {
        int i = 0;
        int j = 0;
        int count = 0;

        while (i < a.length && j < b.length) {
            if (a[i] <= b[j]) {
                count++;
                if (count == k) {
                    return a[i];
                }
                i++;
            } else {
                count++;
                if (count == k) {
                    return b[j];
                }
                j++;
            }
        }

        // If array a is exhausted
        while (i < a.length) {
            count++;
            if (count == k) {
                return a[i];
            }
            i++;
        }

        // If array b is exhausted
        while (j < b.length) {
            count++;
            if (count == k) {
                return b[j];
            }
            j++;
        }

        return -1;
    }
}
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:**
O(k)

We process only the first k elements instead of merging both complete arrays.
**Space Complexity:**
O(1)

No extra array is created.

